  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
